@page "/privacy"
@model PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}
<h1>Privacy</h1>
<p class="muted">How @Model.ServiceName handles personal data, following the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).
    The server is located in Germany.</p>

<h2>Who is responsible</h2>
<div class="panel">
    @if (Model.HasOperator)
    {
        <p><strong>@Model.Web.OperatorName</strong></p>
        @if (Model.Web.OperatorAddress.Length > 0)
        {
            <p>@Model.Web.OperatorAddress</p>
        }
        @if (Model.Web.OperatorContact.Length > 0)
        {
            <p>Contact: @Model.Web.OperatorContact</p>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <p class="muted">The operator of this server has not entered their contact details yet (setting Web:OperatorName,
            Web:OperatorAddress, Web:OperatorContact).</p>
    }
</div>

<h2>What is stored, why and for how long</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Purpose and legal basis</th><th>Kept</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Account: user name, password hash (never the password itself), optional e-mail address, role, status,
                    a hash of your recovery code, profile visibility, creation and last login time</td>
                <td>Providing your account (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
                <td>Until you delete the account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Sessions: a hash of the session token, a device label (game version or browser), the IP address at
                    login and the one it was last used from, times</td>
                <td>Keeping you logged in, showing your active sessions (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
                <td>Until 30 days after the session ended or expired</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Security log: logins, failed logins, account changes, uploads flagged by plausibility checks, friend
                    requests and blocks refused by a limit (which limit, not the other player) and staff actions, each
                    with the IP address. A failed login with a user name that belongs to no account keeps
                    only a keyed hash of the typed name, never the name itself</td>
                <td>Protecting accounts and the service against abuse (Art. 6(1)(f), legitimate interest)</td>
                <td>180 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Online character: the character you play online (as the game saves it), its last 20 revisions and
                    your online play time</td>
                <td>Online play; shown on your profile when it is public (Art. 6(1)(b)); after a reset, undoing a reset
                    made by mistake and reviewing a character the plausibility checks marked (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
                <td>Until you delete the account. When you reset the character, its revisions stay 30 days, then they are
                    deleted; the security log notes the reset and whether the character was marked</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Online story: the story progress of the rooms you open (story flags, key items and two counters of
                    the game), saved with your account, and its last 100 revisions with the time and why each was written.
                    The members of your rooms change it by playing in them (every member's progress counts for the room's
                    owner)</td>
                <td>Running the shared story of your rooms (Art. 6(1)(b)); the revisions let the staff repair a story
                    damaged by misuse or an error (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
                <td>Until you delete the account. Resetting the online character empties it; the earlier revisions stay
                    in the history (the last 100)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Cloud save: your private backup of the local save (latest and 5 older revisions)</td>
                <td>The backup you asked for; never shown to anyone else (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
                <td>Until you delete it or the account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Achievements, play statistics and best times</td>
                <td>Your profile when it is public; rarity percentages count them without names (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
                <td>Until you delete the account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Friends and blocks: who your friends are and since when, the friend requests you sent and received
                    (with the time they were sent and whether one was declined) and the players you block</td>
                <td>The friend list you use (Art. 6(1)(b)); keeping declines and blocks silent protects players from
                    unwanted contact (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
                <td>A friendship until either of you removes it or an account is deleted; a block until the player who
                    set it lifts it or an account is deleted; a request until it is accepted, cancelled by its sender or
                    expires 30 days after it was last sent. A declined request is kept until it expires even when its
                    sender cancels it, so that the decline holds</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Online preferences: the visibility your instances start with, who may invite you, who sees where you
                    play, friend notes in the game, the settings your instances start with; when you were last online</td>
                <td>Playing online as you set it up (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
                <td>Until you change them or delete the account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Instances: who hosted an instance, who played in it (or watched it as staff) and when, its area,
                    visibility, difficulty and settings, and why it ended; while you are online, where you play
                    (relay state, not stored)</td>
                <td>Running the online world, "Recently played with", moderation and checking that achievements happened
                    in online play (Art. 6(1)(b), (f))</td>
                <td>90 days; your membership rows go with your account at once (an instance you hosted stays without
                    your name)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Reports: who reported whom, the reason picked from a fixed list (griefing, cheating, name, other; no
                    free text), whether it came from the game or the website, the instance both played in (when there was
                    one), when it came, and which moderator or admin handled it and when. The relay's technical log notes
                    each report from the game with both account numbers</td>
                <td>Moderation: protecting players against abuse (Art. 6(1)(f), legitimate interest). Moderators and
                    admins see the reports with the reporter's name; the reported player never sees who reported him
                    (a staff member does not see the reporter of a report about himself either)</td>
                <td>Until @Model.ReportDoneDays days after a moderator handled it; an open report until it is handled.
                    Your own reports go with your account at once; reports about you keep only the anonymous placeholder
                    of your account. The technical log's note: 30 days</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Rooms (before the online world): room name, who was a member and when</td>
                <td>The record of earlier achievements (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
                <td>The room record stays until a later update removes rooms; your membership rows go with your account</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Technical log files of the service (errors, instance and relay events)</td>
                <td>Keeping the service running (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
                <td>30 days</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Game traffic between players runs through the relay of this server. It carries the game inputs of a session and is
    not stored.</p>

<h2>Cookies</h2>
<p>The website sets two cookies that are technically necessary: one that keeps you logged in and one that protects
    forms against cross-site request forgery. There is no tracking, no analytics, no advertising and nothing is loaded
    from other servers (no external fonts or scripts).</p>

<h2>Who receives data</h2>
@if (Model.Processors.Count == 0)
{
    <p>Nobody. Data is not sold or shared. The hosting provider of the server processes it only on the operator's behalf.</p>
}
else
{
    <p>Data is not sold. The hosting provider of the server and these service providers process it only on the
        operator's behalf:</p>
    <ul>
        @foreach (var processor in Model.Processors)
        {
            <li>@processor</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
<p>Your user name and, when your profile is public, your online character and achievements are visible to everyone on
    this website. Signed-out visitors see only how many instances run in each area and how many play in them, never a
    name. Signed-in players see the instances they may join: the host's user name, where the host is, how many play
    in it, its difficulty and settings. Private instances are listed to nobody, Friends-only ones only to the host's
    friends, and nothing at all is shown between two players when either blocks the other. In the game, players of the
    same instance see each other's user names, and players nearby see your figure in the lobby. When a player leaves
    the lobby for an area, the game's list of that area's instances shows him, for every instance he may see by the
    rules above, the host's user name and the user names, levels and Digimon of up to four players in it.</p>
<p>Where you play: your friends see whether you are online, where you play (only "Playing" in a Private instance) and
    when you were last online, unless you set "who sees where you play" to nobody in your online preferences. Nobody
    else sees it. "Recently played with" shows you the players you shared an instance with in the last 30 days, and
    shows you to them.</p>
<p>Moderators may join or watch any instance to moderate it, Private ones included; every look and every action
    (ending an instance, removing a player) is logged with their name. A moderator who plays with you carries a Staff
    tag.</p>
<p>Friends: your friends see your user name in their friend list with the date you became friends; a player you send
    a friend request sees your user name with it once it reaches him (at most 100 requests reach a player at a time,
    further ones wait their turn) until he answers or it expires. Nobody else sees who your friends are.
    There is no search for players: a request or a block names the exact user name. Declining a request is not told to
    its sender (it stays "sent" for him until it expires), and a block is never revealed: the blocked player is not
    told, and while you block him his requests to you still look sent to him but never reach you.</p>

<h2>Automated checks</h2>
<p>Uploads of the online character are checked for plausibility. A failed check only marks the upload for the staff
    to look at; nothing is decided or banned automatically. Resetting a marked character starts a new, unmarked one;
    the old one stays visible to the staff for the 30 days above.</p>

<h2>Your rights</h2>
<p>You have the right to access your data (Art. 15), to correct it (Art. 16), to have it deleted (Art. 17), to restrict
    its processing (Art. 18), to receive it in a portable format (Art. 20), and to object to processing based on
    legitimate interest (Art. 21). You can also complain to a data protection supervisory authority (Art. 77), for
    example the one of your German federal state.</p>

<h2>Deleting your data</h2>
<p>You can delete your account at any time in your <a href="~/account">account settings</a>, or ask the operator to do
    it. Deleting removes your online character, your online story and its revisions, cloud save, achievements,
    statistics, best times, friendships, friend requests and blocks (yours and those about you), your online preferences,
    your instance memberships, the reports you sent, sessions and e-mail address at once, and ends an instance you host.
    Reports about you stay with the anonymous placeholder until their period above ends. The account row stays only as an anonymous placeholder ("deleted_" and a number), and the
    security log keeps its entries without your IP address until they expire after 180 days (failed logins typed with
    your user name lose its hash and their IP address too). Database backups roll over
    within about two months.</p>

<p class="muted small">Last updated: 14 November 2026.</p>
